(1912951, 260171, 31443, '<td valign="top">Love this photo! Your daughter is a natural...let her take the glasses to school!</td>', 'Love this photo! Your daughter is a natural...let her take the glasses to school!', '2005-12-01 14:20:46.000000', NULL, 0),
(1913810, 259128, 15811, '<td valign="top">***Greetings from the critique club***<br/> <br/>I think this photo is really well thought out for this particular challenge.  I don''t remember seeing another one like this throughout the whole thing...and that shows me that you have a lot of creativity.<br/> <br/>That being said, there would need to be something <i>out of the ordinary</i> about the photo though to make it really stand up.  I think if you looked up ''color theory'' and maybe read into that a little bit, possibly that could create a different impact to the viewer.<br/> <br/>Compositionally, the photo is decent...probably like a 5 out of 10.  And when I say compositionally, I mean the composition of the whole scene including subject placement and lighting...because lighting is an important part of composition.  I think more lighting from the top would really bring a new dimension to the photo -- that is, bring out the magnifying glass from being just a shadowey object that just so happens to be a magnifying glass to actually being part of the scene.<br/> <br/>--<br/> <br/>Anyway, good work on the photo, and like I said, you have a great eye for this type of thing...keep shooting!</td>', '***Greetings from the critique club*** I think this photo is really well thought out for this particular challenge.  I don''t remember seeing another one like this throughout the whole thing...and that shows me that you have a lot of creativity. That being said, there would need to be something out of the ordinary about the photo though to make it really stand up.  I think if you looked up ''color theory'' and maybe read into that a little bit, possibly that could create a different impact to the viewer. Compositionally, the photo is decent...probably like a 5 out of 10.  And when I say compositionally, I mean the composition of the whole scene including subject placement and lighting...because lighting is an important part of composition.  I think more lighting from the top would really bring a new dimension to the photo -- that is, bring out the magnifying glass from being just a shadowey object that just so happens to be a magnifying glass to actually being part of the scene. -- Anyway, good work on the photo, and like I said, you have a great eye for this type of thing...keep shooting!', '2005-12-01 18:30:49.000000', NULL, 0),
(1913935, 260171, 489, '<td valign="top">Great job Shannon - creative idea.</td>', 'Great job Shannon - creative idea.', '2005-12-01 19:28:30.000000', NULL, 0),
(1914199, 260122, 41802, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club*<br/> <br/>Personally I think "Steroids do not lead to success" is a prefect entry for the Cheater! challenge.  I would say the voters agree as well, I thought it would have been top 10 but hey 11 is pretty darn decent.  What I think grabs my attention the most is the drip on the needle, awesome choice using green.  The green is such a great contrast with the background and liquid in the needle, plus who wants something green injected into them.  The shallow depth of field give a different perspective that really allows the voter to see what the subject is.   Also normally I am not a fan of adding a border but I do think you made the right decision in using one.  I can''t think of anything I would change. Keep up the awesome work! <br/> <br/></td>', '*Critique Club* Personally I think "Steroids do not lead to success" is a prefect entry for the Cheater! challenge.  I would say the voters agree as well, I thought it would have been top 10 but hey 11 is pretty darn decent.  What I think grabs my attention the most is the drip on the needle, awesome choice using green.  The green is such a great contrast with the background and liquid in the needle, plus who wants something green injected into them.  The shallow depth of field give a different perspective that really allows the voter to see what the subject is.   Also normally I am not a fan of adding a border but I do think you made the right decision in using one.  I can''t think of anything I would change. Keep up the awesome work!  ', '2005-12-01 20:57:50.000000', NULL, 0),
(1914341, 260122, 37544, '<td valign="top">I was very impressed with this entry Rikki, its so good. I was one of the 6 who gave it a 10. I thought this would have ribboned.</td>', 'I was very impressed with this entry Rikki, its so good. I was one of the 6 who gave it a 10. I thought this would have ribboned.', '2005-12-01 21:30:54.000000', NULL, 0),
(1914441, 259889, 41802, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club*<br/> <br/>Ok how do I get so lucky, I get some of the best photos to critique.  You did an awesome job and a very well deserved ribbon! The first thing that grabbed me was the head missing, great idea(can you tell I am a girl? My husband of course noticed the legs).  The crop is very unique and demands attention.  I think the hand poses of the model are great, the middle finger is very suttle.   I think a brighter nail polish would have taken away from the overall effect of the shot (like someone had commented).  I really don''t see anything I would change you did an excellent job! Congrats!</td>', '*Critique Club* Ok how do I get so lucky, I get some of the best photos to critique.  You did an awesome job and a very well deserved ribbon! The first thing that grabbed me was the head missing, great idea(can you tell I am a girl? My husband of course noticed the legs).  The crop is very unique and demands attention.  I think the hand poses of the model are great, the middle finger is very suttle.   I think a brighter nail polish would have taken away from the overall effect of the shot (like someone had commented).  I really don''t see anything I would change you did an excellent job! Congrats!', '2005-12-01 21:51:29.000000', NULL, 0),
(1914770, 258782, 41802, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club*<br/> <br/>I remember seeing this shot and thinking it would have scored higher, especially since everyone else had some cards up their sleeves.  I really can''t believe the final average score was less then a 5. I like that you chose to de-saturate as opposed to just going black and white, it gives the shot a little more character in my opinion.  I also really like how you filled the entire frame.  I think the facial expression is very prefect for the Cheater challenge. It would probably been hard for the model to bring the other eyebrow a little lower because of her high brows.  I give her and you credit for letting you get so close.   On my screen I see something on her nose, possibly an eyelash it just looks a little of in a close up like this.  I think this is a very good piece, I just think voters are just wanting to see more of the actual cheating taking place.</td>', '*Critique Club* I remember seeing this shot and thinking it would have scored higher, especially since everyone else had some cards up their sleeves.  I really can''t believe the final average score was less then a 5. I like that you chose to de-saturate as opposed to just going black and white, it gives the shot a little more character in my opinion.  I also really like how you filled the entire frame.  I think the facial expression is very prefect for the Cheater challenge. It would probably been hard for the model to bring the other eyebrow a little lower because of her high brows.  I give her and you credit for letting you get so close.   On my screen I see something on her nose, possibly an eyelash it just looks a little of in a close up like this.  I think this is a very good piece, I just think voters are just wanting to see more of the actual cheating taking place.', '2005-12-01 23:32:18.000000', NULL, 0),
(1914806, 259961, 41802, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club*<br/> <br/>Ok, I get it now.  I thought this was a cool shot during the challenge.  I am much like you and have abstract ideas that people look at and wonder how the heck I got there.  Seeing what you were thinking I love the shot even more.  Excellent night work, I don''t think people know how hard night shots can be.  I love that the subject fills the frame from head to tail, your cropping is prefect. This is really a cool shot, it just might have grabbed more attention in a different category.</td>', '*Critique Club* Ok, I get it now.  I thought this was a cool shot during the challenge.  I am much like you and have abstract ideas that people look at and wonder how the heck I got there.  Seeing what you were thinking I love the shot even more.  Excellent night work, I don''t think people know how hard night shots can be.  I love that the subject fills the frame from head to tail, your cropping is prefect. This is really a cool shot, it just might have grabbed more attention in a different category.', '2005-12-01 23:39:00.000000', NULL, 0),
(1915167, 260115, 39429, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>First Impression:  As I look at the photo and title, I see a man taking off/playing with/putting on his wedding band.  The title leads me to believe he''s taking off the band as an ending to the marriage.  The hands stand out and the ring is definately the point of focus.  There is a strong emotion that comes across in this shot.<br/> <br/>Technical Quality:  The image is sharp and in focus.  Details of the hands and wrists are clear, while the jeans show slightly giving a sense of the body behind the hands.  The lighting is not working in this shot.  It comes across as flat and too direct on the hands as noted by the comments received. The commenters have given some good suggestions for improving the lighting.  It also created an unappealing reflection on the ring. The colors are true to the skin tones and leave no color casts.<br/> <br/>Composition:  You''ve created a strong diagnol on the left side of your photo that works quite well, but stops on the right side.  The fingers create leading lines that take the eye right to the ring.  A crop which placed the ring on the right 1/3 line (cropping out the major part of the ring hand and wrist)probably would have created a stronger, more pleasing image.  The thumb tends to draw the eye away from the image and would look better folded down.  As noted in the comments, the jeans in the lower corner don''t add to this image.<br/> <br/>Emotional Appeal:  You concept is very interesting.  There is a pathos and sadness that comes in looking at the image and the heartbreak it implies. <br/> <br/>Meeting the Challenge: When voting on your image, I had the feeling that I didn''t know if you were putting the ring on after cheating or taking it off to cheat.  I wanted something more in the image to let me know.  A small amount of motion blur at the finger tips or tension on the skin at the knuckle might have given that feel.  When I look at the  title, it takes me away from the "Cheating" theme and gives more of a feel of break-up which takes away from meeting the challenge.  Perhaps a title with a theme such as "Time to Face His Wife" or "Good, There''s No Mark" may have given stronger direction on your take.<br/> <br/>Overall impressions:  I really liked the idea and wanted more from your image.  You were original in your concept and pushed the idea of cheating into moral grounds.  I think your image could be even stronger with a different crop and  lighting.  I''d like to see what this image would look like in black and white, since the color is not an important element of the image, yet the blackness surrounding the hands is important to the emotion.  <br/> <br/>In looking at your portfolio, I see some strong graphic elements in your strongest images.  This image also has the potential for a strong graphic look.  You may wish to consider taking it that direction.  <br/> <br/>I look forward to seeing your future work.  <br/> <br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! First Impression:  As I look at the photo and title, I see a man taking off/playing with/putting on his wedding band.  The title leads me to believe he''s taking off the band as an ending to the marriage.  The hands stand out and the ring is definately the point of focus.  There is a strong emotion that comes across in this shot. Technical Quality:  The image is sharp and in focus.  Details of the hands and wrists are clear, while the jeans show slightly giving a sense of the body behind the hands.  The lighting is not working in this shot.  It comes across as flat and too direct on the hands as noted by the comments received. The commenters have given some good suggestions for improving the lighting.  It also created an unappealing reflection on the ring. The colors are true to the skin tones and leave no color casts. Composition:  You''ve created a strong diagnol on the left side of your photo that works quite well, but stops on the right side.  The fingers create leading lines that take the eye right to the ring.  A crop which placed the ring on the right 1/3 line (cropping out the major part of the ring hand and wrist)probably would have created a stronger, more pleasing image.  The thumb tends to draw the eye away from the image and would look better folded down.  As noted in the comments, the jeans in the lower corner don''t add to this image. Emotional Appeal:  You concept is very interesting.  There is a pathos and sadness that comes in looking at the image and the heartbreak it implies.  Meeting the Challenge: When voting on your image, I had the feeling that I didn''t know if you were putting the ring on after cheating or taking it off to cheat.  I wanted something more in the image to let me know.  A small amount of motion blur at the finger tips or tension on the skin at the knuckle might have given that feel.  When I look at the  title, it takes me away from the "Cheating" theme and gives more of a feel of break-up which takes away from meeting the challenge.  Perhaps a title with a theme such as "Time to Face His Wife" or "Good, There''s No Mark" may have given stronger direction on your take. Overall impressions:  I really liked the idea and wanted more from your image.  You were original in your concept and pushed the idea of cheating into moral grounds.  I think your image could be even stronger with a different crop and  lighting.  I''d like to see what this image would look like in black and white, since the color is not an important element of the image, yet the blackness surrounding the hands is important to the emotion.   In looking at your portfolio, I see some strong graphic elements in your strongest images.  This image also has the potential for a strong graphic look.  You may wish to consider taking it that direction.   I look forward to seeing your future work.   ', '2005-12-02 02:37:05.000000', NULL, 0),
(1915337, 259889, 5885, '<td valign="top">Reminds me of highschool and trying to cheat off the cheerleader in the next desk ;-) Great shot. Congrats on the ribbon/</td>', 'Reminds me of highschool and trying to cheat off the cheerleader in the next desk ;-) Great shot. Congrats on the ribbon/', '2005-12-02 05:06:03.000000', NULL, 0),
(1915339, 259735, 5885, '<td valign="top">Come a long way from the bushes huh? LOL ... congrats on the ribbon.</td>', 'Come a long way from the bushes huh? LOL ... congrats on the ribbon.', '2005-12-02 05:08:41.000000', NULL, 0),
(1915357, 259848, 27482, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the critique club.<br/> <br/>I haven´t seen this image before, didn´t vote but I probably would have given this a 4 to be honest.  Technically it´s not bad but hardly good either.  The lighting is a bit flat and the bland black backround isn´t helping, I personally would have liked this shot heaps more if the backround had some texture, like a wooden table or something like that.  I notice you used an extremely small aperture for this shot and a long shutter speed.  The shutter speed is pretty much irrelevant since the subject isn´t moving but I don´t really understand the aperture.  Was it really neccesary to have everything inside the DOF?  I seriously doubt it so I would suggest you keep to f5.6-11 to make the most out of the sharpness of your lens, at small apertures the lens starts to suffer sharpness due to diffraction and I think it shows in the shot.  As a matter of fact, did you sharpen after resizing it, the shot looks a little bit too soft.<br/> <br/>On the good side, the composition is decent, not really an interesting angle but nothing bad about it at all.  The shot is pretty well exposed, seem to have a few blown out highlights though so softer lighting would be a key to reduce that.  Also the shot is in focus, no question although I personally would have used a bigger aperture.<br/> <br/>Certainly meets the challenge but you really have to keep in mind that most people just look at the image for a second and then go on to the next one and you really have to look for all five aces.  I would suggest keeping it more simple next time, like having a hand hold all fice aces so that the point comes across right away, it should lead to a better rating.<br/> <br/>Hope I wasn´t too negative, I wouldn´t give you such a hard time if I didn´t see potential so keep it up :D<br/> <br/>Kind regards, Larus.<br/> <br/></td>', 'Greetings from the critique club. I haven´t seen this image before, didn´t vote but I probably would have given this a 4 to be honest.  Technically it´s not bad but hardly good either.  The lighting is a bit flat and the bland black backround isn´t helping, I personally would have liked this shot heaps more if the backround had some texture, like a wooden table or something like that.  I notice you used an extremely small aperture for this shot and a long shutter speed.  The shutter speed is pretty much irrelevant since the subject isn´t moving but I don´t really understand the aperture.  Was it really neccesary to have everything inside the DOF?  I seriously doubt it so I would suggest you keep to f5.6-11 to make the most out of the sharpness of your lens, at small apertures the lens starts to suffer sharpness due to diffraction and I think it shows in the shot.  As a matter of fact, did you sharpen after resizing it, the shot looks a little bit too soft. On the good side, the composition is decent, not really an interesting angle but nothing bad about it at all.  The shot is pretty well exposed, seem to have a few blown out highlights though so softer lighting would be a key to reduce that.  Also the shot is in focus, no question although I personally would have used a bigger aperture. Certainly meets the challenge but you really have to keep in mind that most people just look at the image for a second and then go on to the next one and you really have to look for all five aces.  I would suggest keeping it more simple next time, like having a hand hold all fice aces so that the point comes across right away, it should lead to a better rating. Hope I wasn´t too negative, I wouldn´t give you such a hard time if I didn´t see potential so keep it up :D Kind regards, Larus. ', '2005-12-02 05:40:35.000000', NULL, 0),
(1915398, 259834, 27482, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the critique club :)<br/> <br/>I haven´t seen this image before since I didn´t vote in this challenge but to be honest I probably would have given it a 4, mainly since the image quality isn´t terribly good and the whole shot has a "snapshot feel" to it.  I assume it´s mostly because you shot this through the front window, frankly it shows as the whole shot is a bit "muddy" for lack of a better word.  No idea on how to improve that though, wouldn´t wanna suggest you stick your head out the window for photography while driving :D<br/> <br/>I am not really fond of the cool WB, I personally would have liked this more if it were slightly more neutral, BUT, it does make the taillights stand out more so if that was your reasoning then it worked very well.<br/> <br/>Exposure is a little dark but kindof fits the shot so no qualms about that.  Composition isn´t bad at all but not really interesting either, hard to see how you could have improved that though sitting behind the wheel.<br/> <br/>Certainly meets the challenge but I am not really familiar with american lingo I didn´t get it at first so for me personally it wasn´t obvious that it referred to the jeep crossing the line to enter another lane (I assume that is what point of the shot is, please keep in mind that english is only my third language)<br/> <br/>Anyway, hope I wasn´t too negative.<br/> <br/>Kind regards, Larus.<br/></td>', 'Greetings from the critique club :) I haven´t seen this image before since I didn´t vote in this challenge but to be honest I probably would have given it a 4, mainly since the image quality isn´t terribly good and the whole shot has a "snapshot feel" to it.  I assume it´s mostly because you shot this through the front window, frankly it shows as the whole shot is a bit "muddy" for lack of a better word.  No idea on how to improve that though, wouldn´t wanna suggest you stick your head out the window for photography while driving :D I am not really fond of the cool WB, I personally would have liked this more if it were slightly more neutral, BUT, it does make the taillights stand out more so if that was your reasoning then it worked very well. Exposure is a little dark but kindof fits the shot so no qualms about that.  Composition isn´t bad at all but not really interesting either, hard to see how you could have improved that though sitting behind the wheel. Certainly meets the challenge but I am not really familiar with american lingo I didn´t get it at first so for me personally it wasn´t obvious that it referred to the jeep crossing the line to enter another lane (I assume that is what point of the shot is, please keep in mind that english is only my third language) Anyway, hope I wasn´t too negative. Kind regards, Larus.', '2005-12-02 07:00:26.000000', NULL, 0),
